00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.460
It’s pretty fun to watch organisms eat in the&nbsp;
microcosmos. There’s a whole range of methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.740
to enjoy. You’ve got single-celled organisms&nbsp;
slowly absorbing prey into their bodies through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:50.580
phagocytosis, rotifers whirling the crown of&nbsp;
cilia on their head to get food into their mouths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:57.144
and so many other techniques that shape the life,&nbsp;
and death, of our invisible little friends.&nbsp;

00:00:57.144 --> 00:01:03.600
And at the core of all this is a simple,&nbsp;
universal need: energy, energy stored chemically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.504
as adenosine triphosphate—or ATP—that’s&nbsp;
made from the breakdown of sugars and fats.&nbsp;

00:01:10.504 --> 00:01:17.160
In bacteria, the production of ATP happens&nbsp;
at the cell surface membrane, with protons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:24.246
and electrons traveling at the borders of the&nbsp;
organism to drive the creation of this fuel.&nbsp;

00:01:24.246 --> 00:01:30.239
And the process is similar for eukaryotes. Except&nbsp;
unlike prokaryotes, eukaryotes have organelles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.239 --> 00:01:35.418
specialized bodies inside of them to&nbsp;
carry out special chemical reactions.

00:01:35.418 --> 00:01:39.780
 And there is even an&nbsp;organelle dedicated 
to making ATP. Perhaps you have

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.420
heard about it? The mitochondrion? Better&nbsp;
known as the powerhouse of the cell.&nbsp;

00:01:48.515 --> 00:01:50.520
Yes the powerhouse of the cell.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:57.180
The origins of that phrase seems to come from&nbsp;
a 1957 Scientific American article written by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:03.300
Philip Siekevitz that was titled “Powerhouse&nbsp;
of the Cell.” It’s gone on to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:09.780
such a ubiquitous way to describe the organelle&nbsp;
that it even has an entry on knowyourmeme.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:15.840
which says, quote, “the phrase is typically&nbsp;
mocked as an example of impractical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:21.480
information taught in public schools.”
Obviously, we at Journey to the Microcosmos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:27.120
would never mock an organelle, especially&nbsp;
one that is making it possible for us to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.100
just about every single thing we are doing&nbsp;
at this moment. But we would like to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:40.560
a moment to maybe, lightly question some things.
Because is the mitochondria always the powerhouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:47.520
of the cell? No. Not for all eukaryotes.&nbsp;
And this matters because it shapes the way we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.800
understand the evolution of eukaryotes.
There’s a story about giant amoebas that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.940
we’ve shared before but we want to talk about it&nbsp;
in more detail today because when you’re talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:03.420
about exceptions and nuances to a commonly stated&nbsp;
scientific fact, those details are fascinating.&nbsp;

00:03:04.057 --> 00:03:07.740
We also want to talk about it because&nbsp;
we want to talk about diplomonads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:12.060
and to share, again, one of our favorite&nbsp;
historical observations of the microcosmos.&nbsp;

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:17.700
In the 17th century, the original master&nbsp;
of microscopes Antoni Von Leeuwenhoeck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:23.820
woke up with some, less than ideal bowel&nbsp;
movements. But Leeuwenhoek knew a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:29.933
opportunity when he saw one, and he took his&nbsp;
watery sample to the microscope. He wrote:&nbsp;

00:03:29.933 --> 00:03:35.340
I have at times seen very prettily&nbsp;
moving animalcules. Their bodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.720
were somewhat longer than broad, and&nbsp;
their belly, which was flattened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.000
provided with several feet, with which they&nbsp;
made such a movement through the clear medium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.520
and the globules that we might fancy we&nbsp;
saw a pissabed running up against a wall.&nbsp;

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:57.000
Clifford Dobell, Leeuwenhoek’s biographer, took&nbsp;
this description and matched it to the medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:02.520
knowledge available to him in the early 20th&nbsp;
century to diagnose Leeuwenhoek with giardiasis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:06.840
a disease borne out a parasitic diplomonad&nbsp;
that colonizes the human intestine.&nbsp;

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:13.380
Now not all diplomonads are parasitic, but they&nbsp;
are all just a little bit weird. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:18.480
they have two nuclei. They also have eight&nbsp;
flagella trailing behind and around them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.520
as they spin through the microcosmos.
What makes diplomonads really weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:30.660
is that they do not have: mitochondria. This&nbsp;
puts diplomonads into a small and exclusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:35.760
club with a handful of other eukaryotes.&nbsp;
These organisms, which scientists grouped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.340
together into what they called the Archezoa, are&nbsp;
anaerobic, meaning they don’t need oxygen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.020
might even prefer to live away from it.
And that would explain why they might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.872
need mitochondria, which require&nbsp;
oxygen to drive their reactions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.872 --> 00:04:57.375
But it does not explain how they got that way.
So scientists worked with the information they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.375 --> 00:05:03.699
had at the time to come up with an explanation, an&nbsp;
explanation that would later be proven wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:09.480
They reasoned that diplomonads and their fellow&nbsp;
Archezoans got that way because they just appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:15.638
a little too early. Mitochondria are thought to&nbsp;
have been the result of an endosymbiotic event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.638 --> 00:05:21.338
where at one point in eukaryotic&nbsp;
history, one cell engulfed a prokaryote.&nbsp;

00:05:21.338 --> 00:05:27.000
But instead of getting digested and consumed,&nbsp;
this little prokaryote inside of another cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.840
became useful in a different way. It became&nbsp;
an organelle. And the diplomonads, scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.380
thought, just missed out on that big event.
Except, it turned out that the Archezoa might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:45.480
have gotten in on the mitochondria party, they&nbsp;
just also left it, losing their&nbsp;mitochondria

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:51.180
to evolution’s inventive ways.&nbsp;
In the process, the Archezoa were left with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:57.540
genetic mementos from their former organelle.
One of the unique facets of mitochondria is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:02.820
that they have their own DNA, separate from&nbsp;
the DNA of a cell’s nucleus but still able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:09.000
instruct the production of proteins necessary&nbsp;
to the mitochondria. But what became clear is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.464
that there wasn’t nearly enough DNA&nbsp;
to encode for all of those proteins.&nbsp;

00:06:13.464 --> 00:06:18.540
In fact, the mitochondria only produce a&nbsp;
small fraction of the proteins they need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:25.380
around 15% according to one estimate. The rest&nbsp;
are encoded by DNA in the nucleus, transferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:32.156
there at some point by the endosymbiotic&nbsp;
prokaryote that would become the mitochondria.&nbsp;

00:06:32.156 --> 00:06:37.860
This fact became important when scientists&nbsp;
realized that some of these genes could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:44.460
found in diplomonads and their fellow Archezoa.&nbsp;
Genes that encoded proteins usually associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:50.100
with the mitochondria existed in organisms that&nbsp;
were thought to have missed out on mitochondria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:56.280
entirely. Which meant that perhaps they&nbsp;
had not missed out on mitochondria. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.900
they had had mitochondria and then simply&nbsp;
traded them for a different way of life.&nbsp;

00:07:01.566 --> 00:07:06.169
Now while there was always the possibility that these&nbsp;
genes could have come from some other source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:11.760
that alternative seemed increasingly unlikely&nbsp;
as it became clear that Archezoa have organelles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:17.940
similar to mitochondria, just rewired to suit&nbsp;
the chemical needs of these anaerobic organisms.&nbsp;

00:07:18.486 --> 00:07:25.020
In the diplomonads, the organelle is called the&nbsp;
mitosome. But curiously, despite the resemblance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:31.560
to mitochondria, the mitosome doesn’t actually&nbsp;
make ATP. It’s mostly known to make clusters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:38.438
of iron and sulfur used in many cellular&nbsp;
functions, including metabolism. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.438 --> 00:07:44.616
diplomonads make ATP in their cytosol, that&nbsp;
soupy mix that surrounds their organelles.&nbsp;

00:07:44.616 --> 00:07:49.740
Other members of what was once called the Archezoa&nbsp;
have their own mitochondria-like organelles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:55.680
capable of making ATP. And the result has&nbsp;
been a challenge to the story previously told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:01.875
about them, that these were early progenitors of&nbsp;
eukaryotes, simpler than what would come after.&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:09.780
If anything, it seems to be the opposite. These&nbsp;
organisms likely evolved with mitochondria like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:15.000
their fellow eukaryotes, but they also&nbsp;
evolved out of them. Which means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:21.900
there are so many other questions to explore.
Questions about how organisms found their way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:26.940
and survived in anaerobic conditions.&nbsp;
Questions about how mitochondria have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:33.000
evolved. And questions about what the origins&nbsp;
of eukaryotic life would have looked like.&nbsp;

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:38.520
And as for the diplomonad, I suppose we should&nbsp;
say that the cytosol is the powerhouse of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:43.920
cell. And the point is not to poke holes in a&nbsp;
popular biology aphorism taught to students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:50.179
The mitochondria is the powerhouse of the cell&nbsp;
for many, many organisms, including you.

00:08:50.179 --> 00:08:54.120
But when it comes to evolution, the&nbsp;
exceptions to the rules we think define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:00.840
us are the source of incredible futures&nbsp;
written in mysterious and varied pasts.

00:09:05.738 --> 00:09:10.736
